      "hints": [
        "For parametric curves, use $dy/dx=(dy/dt)/(dx/dt)$.",
        "Differentiate $x=2t+\\sin2t$ and use the chain rule on $y=\\ln(1-\\cos2t)$.",
        "Form the quotient and factor $1+\\cos2t$ and $1-\\cos2t$ using half-angle identities.",
        "Cancel carefully to reduce the expression to the reciprocal of $\\sin2t$."
      ],
      "walkthrough": [
        "Given the parametric equations $x = 2t + \\sin 2t$ and $y = \\ln(1 - \\cos 2t)$.",
        "Differentiate $x$ with respect to $t$:\n$$\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}x}{\\mathrm{d}t} = 2 + 2\\cos 2t = 2(1 + \\cos 2t)$$",
        "Differentiate $y$ with respect to $t$ using the chain rule $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\ln(u) = \\dfrac{u'}{u}$:\n$$\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}t} = \\dfrac{-(-\\sin 2t \\cdot 2)}{1 - \\cos 2t} = \\dfrac{2\\sin 2t}{1 - \\cos 2t}$$",
        "Apply the parametric differentiation rule $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x} = \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}y/\\mathrm{d}t}{\\mathrm{d}x/\\mathrm{d}t}$:\n$$\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x} = \\dfrac{\\dfrac{2\\sin 2t}{1 - \\cos 2t}}{2(1 + \\cos 2t)} = \\dfrac{2\\sin 2t}{2(1 - \\cos 2t)(1 + \\cos 2t)} = \\dfrac{\\sin 2t}{(1 - \\cos 2t)(1 + \\cos 2t)}$$",
        "Multiply out the denominator using difference of two squares:\n$$(1 - \\cos 2t)(1 + \\cos 2t) = 1 - \\cos^2 2t$$",
        "Apply the Pythagorean identity $1 - \\cos^2 2t = \\sin^2 2t$:\n$$\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x} = \\dfrac{\\sin 2t}{\\sin^2 2t} = \\dfrac{1}{\\sin 2t} = \\operatorname{cosec} 2t$$\nThis completes the proof."
      ],
